<resources>
    <!--
      The brand, for the frame Android draws BEFORE JavaScript exists.

      Without this the launcher shows a white sheet for the second or so it
      takes to start the runtime, and the app appears to flash before it opens.
      The colour has to be duplicated here because a native resource cannot
      read `src/theme/tokens.ts` — see `src/common/brand.ts` for the rest of
      what a rename has to touch.

      THE SHOPPING SIDE'S OCEAN, and getting this wrong was a real bug rather
      than a preference. This app has two palettes and `palettes.ts` decides
      which side wears which: shopping is MEADOW, working is CARMINE. Android
      cannot ask — this frame is painted before the process exists, let alone
      the store — so it is painted for the answer true of almost everybody and
      stated by the store listing from its first frame: customer.

      It was once #E94E00, and a shopper tapped an orange icon, waited behind
      an orange sheet and arrived in a green app. `splashAndIntro.test.ts`
      asserts this hex against `meadowThemes.light.primary` so the two cannot
      drift again.
    -->
    <color name="brand">#1A759F</color>
    <!--
      THE LAUNCHER ICON'S GROUND — the LOGO's teal, not the app's primary.

      These were the same hex, on the reasoning that "the icon's ground, the
      window and the splash are one fact in three files". That held while the
      icon was a glyph this repo drew. It is supplied artwork now: a rounded
      square whose ground is its own deep teal, with the T mark on it, and a
      launcher icon is the one place the ARTWORK decides rather than the
      palette.

      The two are still one FAMILY — #0B4A50 against the splash's #1A759F —
      so the original complaint is still answered. That complaint was never
      "these hexes differ"; it was a green icon opening an orange app, 150
      degrees away on the hue wheel. These are 8 degrees apart.
    -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#0B4A50</color>
    <color name="brand_dark">#1E6091</color>
</resources>
